.TH RBAC\-AUDIT 1 "2026-09" "rbac-auditor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rbac-audit \- snapshot Kubernetes RBAC and diff it under a policy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rbac-audit snapshot
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B rbac-audit diff
.I OLD
.RI [ NEW ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Writes a cluster's Roles, ClusterRoles, RoleBindings, ClusterRoleBindings and
ServiceAccounts to a deterministic JSON file meant to be committed to a
repository, and compares two such files \(em or one against the live cluster
\(em under a policy that decides the exit status.
.PP
Point-in-time questions ("who can delete pods", access matrices, listing every
wildcard grant) are deliberately not here. Use
.BR rakkess ,
.B rbac-tool
or
.B rbac-lookup
for those.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B snapshot
Dump RBAC as deterministic JSON: sorted, normalised, and with no timestamp in
the payload, so an unchanged cluster produces byte-identical output. The file
declares
.BR kind:\ RbacSnapshot ,
which is what distinguishes it from the
.B \-\-json
diff report.
.TP
.BI diff " OLD " [ NEW ]
Compare two snapshots, or
.I OLD
against the live cluster when
.I NEW
is omitted. Prints a readable diff, applies the policy and exits 2 if it fails.
Only newly allowed permissions are judged: removing a grant, or narrowing one,
never fails the build.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output " PATH"
Where
.B snapshot
writes.
.B \-
is standard output, which is the default.
.TP
.BI \-\-context " NAME"
The kubeconfig context to read. Only meaningful when a cluster is consulted.
.TP
.BI \-\-policy " PATH"
The policy file. Defaults to
.I .rbac-policy.yaml
in the working directory if it exists, and to the built-in rules otherwise.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-policy
Report what the policy would block without acting on it: every rule still
runs and every violation is still printed, marked
.BR (warn) ,
but the exit status stays 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-subject " KIND/NAME"
Scope the diff to one subject and render it as a resource \(mu verb matrix with
additions and removals marked.
.I KIND
is ServiceAccount, User or Group. A ServiceAccount needs its namespace, as
.IR ServiceAccount/ namespace / name ;
a User or Group has none and must not be given one.
.TP
.BI \-\-json " PATH"
Also write the machine-readable diff.
.B \-
sends it to standard output and suppresses the human report, so the pipe stays
parseable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .rbac-policy.yaml
The policy: which additions fail the build, and which objects are exempt from
that. See
.I .rbac-policy.example.yaml
in the distribution.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No policy violations, or every one of them exempted.
.TP
.B 2
At least one policy violation.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error: a missing operand, an unknown flag, a malformed \-\-subject.
.TP
.B 65
A snapshot or policy file that could not be parsed.
.TP
.B 66
A file named on the command line does not exist.
.TP
.B 69
.BR kubectl (1)
could not reach the cluster, or is not installed.
.TP
.B 73
An output file could not be written.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Take a snapshot and commit it:
.PP
.RS
.B rbac-audit snapshot \-o rbac/prod.json
.RE
.PP
Gate CI on drift against the live cluster:
.PP
.RS
.B rbac-audit diff rbac/prod.json
.RE
.PP
Two files, no cluster access:
.PP
.RS
.B rbac-audit diff last-week.json this-week.json
.RE
.PP
What one ServiceAccount gained:
.PP
.RS
.B rbac-audit diff last-week.json \-\-subject ServiceAccount/ci/deployer
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kubectl (1).
Project documentation:
https://github.com/fabiocicerchia/rbac-auditor
